{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1124 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "2212cb6fe53e428eacbd8175d2ad735e892f5495bdf598d8f2b2100c7f4837cc" :edb/db-before-t 1123 :edb/db-after-t 1124 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534436 50 #inst "2026-10-07T03:44:47.913Z" 13194139534436 true] [17592186184092 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288122 13194139534436 true] [17592186184092 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288143 13194139534436 true] [17592186184093 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288129 13194139534436 true] [17592186184093 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288136 13194139534436 true] [17592186184093 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288150 13194139534436 true] [17592186288122 1017 #edb/ref 17592186288123 13194139534436 true] [17592186288122 1089 "q-117798" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288122 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534436 true] [17592186288122 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/assets/454c2746d17f6444a9fa5762baae3927fb72eff6a7e51a1df38bc4f325a4a830.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288122 1113 #uuid "7b5e1232-2af8-533d-8946-1db7e40dfb1f" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288122 1145 "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-0=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $2$ units up. Hence, $D=2.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{3}{2}+2=\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})+2.\n$$" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288123 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288124 13194139534436 true] [17592186288123 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288125 13194139534436 true] [17592186288123 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288126 13194139534436 true] [17592186288123 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288127 13194139534436 true] [17592186288123 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288128 13194139534436 true] [17592186288123 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534436 true] [17592186288123 1041 "selection" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288123 1104 #edb/ref 17592186288124 13194139534436 true] [17592186288123 1110 #uuid "95bc63e3-9e9b-5b36-9517-c8f886b958a6" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288124 1034 "\\frac{7}{2}" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288124 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534436 true] [17592186288124 1111 #uuid "c7f1433a-d16c-5912-b99a-14916f963a18" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288125 1034 "\\frac{11}{4}" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288125 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534436 true] [17592186288125 1111 #uuid "2a5bdf80-8c20-5542-afe4-efdc648d57e3" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288126 1034 "3" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288126 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534436 true] [17592186288126 1111 #uuid "5177fa95-c1d1-5de1-8121-d1d408813501" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288127 1034 "\\frac{11}{2}" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288127 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534436 true] [17592186288127 1111 #uuid "b0b6253c-9462-5455-9855-2849b9b0b77c" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288128 1034 "4" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288128 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534436 true] [17592186288128 1111 #uuid "e1eb8646-9cc3-597e-953d-548a532e11a7" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288129 1017 #edb/ref 17592186288130 13194139534436 true] [17592186288129 1089 "q-117888" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288129 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534436 true] [17592186288129 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/assets/5819e0605dacfb190c2767c4f50a671eb592832aa6a3a1970a8bcdff2790fd19.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288129 1113 #uuid "6101e7db-17ef-5f41-b6aa-dedb998ac867" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288129 1145 "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/assets/ad5bf0001a37cf5bebf3288ffbf1fa6bb69747f28f8a81d08d0ffab8223e1707.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{21}-0=\\frac{\\pi }{21}$ units to the right. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{21}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{7}.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{7}.$" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288130 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288131 13194139534436 true] [17592186288130 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288132 13194139534436 true] [17592186288130 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288133 13194139534436 true] [17592186288130 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288134 13194139534436 true] [17592186288130 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288135 13194139534436 true] [17592186288130 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534436 true] [17592186288130 1041 "selection" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288130 1104 #edb/ref 17592186288135 13194139534436 true] [17592186288130 1110 #uuid "29f671bf-57cb-597c-8402-bd70656d18fe" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288131 1034 "\\frac{\\pi }{14}" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288131 1103 #edb/ref 175921