orname{tan}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/assets/cac3ea6b8409290b6716a194325a885672e06572c1f8a8b63b48b6807f514d42.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ units to the left. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288137 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288138 13194139534436 true] [17592186288137 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288139 13194139534436 true] [17592186288137 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288140 13194139534436 true] [17592186288137 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288141 13194139534436 true] [17592186288137 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288142 13194139534436 true] [17592186288137 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534436 true] [17592186288137 1041 "selection" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288137 1104 #edb/ref 17592186288142 13194139534436 true] [17592186288137 1110 #uuid "dd3f3631-d87b-5f00-8744-a6c4890e026d" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288138 1034 "-\\frac{\\pi }{12}" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288138 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534436 true] [17592186288138 1111 #uuid "487b38bc-ff16-52d2-aa97-b5843ab8b967" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288139 1034 "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288139 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534436 true] [17592186288139 1111 #uuid "90ed00e1-1f2f-5683-bc81-f6670ac548d6" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288140 1034 "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288140 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534436 true] [17592186288140 1111 #uuid "4a8ffaae-da7e-535a-9cc5-ea8f3f208dc0" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288141 1034 "\\frac{\\pi }{12}" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288141 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534436 true] [17592186288141 1111 #uuid "0f1cb54b-2ac8-5046-ad4a-07cb6479bbc9" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288142 1034 "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288142 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534436 true] [17592186288142 1111 #uuid "35712184-4acb-5776-8445-4bc98d8c9b6a" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288143 1017 #edb/ref 17592186288144 13194139534436 true] [17592186288143 1089 "q-143669" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288143 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534436 true] [17592186288143 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/assets/f86984919b15283134e1b3a9479ff8c79ec8dfacc84975e82f88704b7b26dd3a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is  $B-2D?$" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288143 1113 #uuid "d39218d1-1972-5152-bee8-516235be3a28" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288143 1145 "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=4.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{9}{4}$ unit down. Therefore, $D=-\\frac{9}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, $B-2D=4-2\\cdot (-\\frac{9}{4})=4+\\frac{9}{2}=\\frac{17}{2}.$  \n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x)-\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288144 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288145 13194139534436 true] [17592186288144 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288146 13194139534436 true] [17592186288144 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288147 13194139534436 true] [17592186288144 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288148 13194139534436 true] [17592186288144 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288149 13194139534436 true] [17592186288144 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534436 true] [17592186288144 1041 "selection" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288144 1104 #edb/ref 17592186288149 13194139534436 true] [17592186288144 1110 #uuid "e8ec1bad-0938-5980-8f74-c9e1b9eeb5f4" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288145 1034 "\\frac{5}{2}" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288145 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534436 true] [17592186288145 1111 #uuid "5b2dd5e5-8108-59f7-8094-2d139667eb3f" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288146 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288146 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534436 true] [17592186288146 1111 #uuid "97ef6dd8-4c5b-5809-9122-4f24c7e9f343" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288147 1034 "\\frac{13}{2}" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288147 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534436 true] [17592186288147 1111 #uuid "b65f0fbf-db07-5593-ab33-61d4f6a2c48e" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288148 1034 "-\\frac{7}{2}" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288148 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534436 true] [17592186288148 1111 #uuid "d48bad41-6151-5d6d-b552-122ed5656569" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288149 1034 "\\frac{17}{2}" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288149 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534436 true] [17592186288149 1111 #uuid "a64e72d4-2879-518d-9201-979fab7adaab" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288150 1017 #edb/ref 17592186288151 13194139534436 true] [17592186288150 1089 "q-143671" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288150 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534436 true] [17592186288150 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/assets/b29154c183a57397bba6050ad02e543ad47e4b979c77435c9f6e88a49e32783e.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288150 1113 #uuid "05c8c3e7-eede-5ba7-84d2-9182c0468743" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288150 1145 "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/assets/595222a0a6987c1fae1a7b245d2936eba08312357015cc6f78c66eb60f942707.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)$ by $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288151 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288152 13194139534436 true] [17592186288151 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288153 13194139534436 true] [17592186288151 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288154 13194139534436 true] [17592186288151 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288155 13194139534436 true] [17592186288151 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288156 13194139534436 true] [17592186288151 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534436 true] [17592186288151 1041 "selection" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288151 1104 #edb/ref 17592186288153 13194139534436 true] [17592186288151 1110 #uuid "fd2223db-c1d3-583f-b0bf-26ceecc64031" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288152 1034 "-\\frac{\\pi }{12}" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288152 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534436 true] [17592186288152 1111 #uuid "b1a99b1e-a904-5335-9e57-816a9d712f71" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288153 1034 "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" 13194139534436 true] [17592186288153 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534436 true] [17592186288153 1111 #uui